---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Showcase from "../../components/Showcase.astro";
import { tokens } from "../../lib/tokens";

import "../../examples/styling-hook-override.css";
import overrideSource from "../../examples/styling-hook-override.css?raw";

const buttonHooks = tokens.filter((t) => t.file === "components/button.css");

const naive = `/* What we DON'T do: one styling hook per variant. */
--sk-button-primary-bg
--sk-button-danger-bg
--sk-button-ghost-bg
--sk-button-primary-bg-hover
--sk-button-danger-bg-hover
/* …and your CSS has to branch between them. */`;

const actual = `/* What we do: a styling hook, re-declared for specificity. */
.sk-button {
  --sk-button-bg: var(--color-action-neutral);
}
.sk-button[data-variant="primary"] {
  --sk-button-bg: var(--color-action-primary);
}
.sk-button[data-variant="danger"] {
  --sk-button-bg: var(--color-action-danger);
}`;

const consumer = `/* Your CSS reads it once and never branches. */
.sk-button { background: var(--sk-button-bg); }`;
---

<Base
  title="The styling hooks contract"
  description="A styling hook is the public knob for a component: what it means, how they are named, and how to step on them."
>
  <h1>The styling hooks contract</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Importing <code>components/button.css</code> gives you a button <em>and</em>
    {buttonHooks.length} styling hooks. Class is what you see; hooks are how you change it without
    touch a selector. That is the contract.
  </p>

  <div class="sk-alert" data-tone="info" data-presentation="accent">
    <div class="sk-alert__content">
    <p>
      This <strong>changed</strong>. For a long time the system sent the hooks and nothing else: the
      structure was written by each consumer. It was reversed, because it did not hold,
      <a href="/decisiones/0008-que-envia-tier-3">decision 8</a> tells what was measured. What not
      What changed is why a hook exists: it is not a replacement for the missing structure, it is the knob.
    </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Why hooks and not one class per variant</h2>
  <p>
    Because a component has a base look, variants and sizes, and those combinations multiply.
    A styling hook collapses them: it is a custom property, and a custom property does not care what
    element carries it nor how many selectors re-declare it. Also, what you do not care about
    <strong>no weight</strong>: a consumer that does not send buttons does not download a single byte of
    <code>button.css</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Styling hooks lose group segment</h2>
  <p>
    Naive coding gives each variant its own variable, and ends in an explosion that
    your CSS has to disambiguate with conditionals:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={naive} label="what we do not do" />

  <p>
    Instead, the base and each variant declare <strong>the same</strong> styling hook. The variant
    you just re-declare it in a more specific selector and <strong>wins for specificity</strong>:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={actual} label="components/button.css" />
  <CodeBlock code={consumer} label="CSS" note="one line, zero branches" />

  <h2>That is why stepping on a styling hook is trivial and total</h2>
  <p>
    Since all variants read the same styling hook, one line restyles them all within a scope.
    And there is no specificity fight because any rule without a layer beats all layers.
  </p>

  <Showcase code={overrideSource} lang="css" label="src/examples/styling-hook-override.css" note="the override that paints the demo">
    {/* `.promo` is the override's scope (`.promo .sk-button`); display:contents keeps the scope while
        letting the buttons lay out in the Showcase stage's own flow. */}
    <div class="promo" style="display: contents">
      <button class="sk-button sk-interactive" data-sk-button data-variant="primary">Save</button>
      <button class="sk-button sk-interactive" data-sk-button data-variant="neutral">Cancel</button>
      <button class="sk-button sk-interactive" data-sk-button data-variant="danger">Delete</button>
    </div>
  </Showcase>

  <p>
    The three buttons above are the same variants of the home button. Three lines restyled them to
    all, without touching the design system and without inventing a new token.
  </p>

  <h2>The risk of specificity</h2>
  <p>
    Variants are gained by selector specificity. A consumer writing one more selector
    specific can step on a variant inadvertently. We accept that risk to maintain few styling
    hooks, without nesting them by variant.
  </p>

  <h2>All button styling hooks</h2>
  <p>
    These are the {buttonHooks.length} sent by <code>components/button.css</code>. They live in
    <code>.sk-button</code>, never in <code>:root</code>, do not exist until an element chooses
    them.
  </p>

  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Hook</th>
          <th>Apunta a</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          buttonHooks.map((t) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="mono">{t.name}</td>
              <td class="mono">{t.value}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Note that none point to a <code>--ramp-*</code>. It is not discipline: it is
    <a href="/en/tiers">the tier-skip rule</a>, and breaks the build.
  </p>
</Base>
